On screen is more than in a window: send the disappear pair when a view is detached or hidden.

_ciderSendAppearance was driven ONLY by the view joining or leaving a window. macOS also sends
viewWillDisappear and viewDidDisappear when the view is hidden, or lifted out of the hierarchy while
the window stays. A controller that commits its state in viewWillDisappear therefore never committed
anything here, silently.

That is not hypothetical for the roster. iA Writer IMPLEMENTS viewWillDisappear on
IAEditorViewController, next to commitRangeChangesToDocumentIfNeeded and
viewWillPerformAnimatedTransition; the 163 method dump that says so came from CIDER_SPY_DUMP,
because the class lives in a stripped Swift binary with no ObjC symbols.

A view is now on screen when it has a window AND neither it nor any ancestor is hidden, and the
whole SUBTREE is notified, because hiding or detaching one view takes every controller below it off
screen too and each of those is owed the same pair. Announced from viewDidMoveToWindow,
removeFromSuperview and setHidden:, the last after the flag is settled and the view has been told,
so anything the controller does sees the state it is being told about.

MEASURED on an iA Writer drive that clicks a file row: viewDidDisappear goes from 0 to 1 in the run.

BE CLEAR ABOUT WHAT IT DID NOT DO. The editor pane is still empty, len 0 in all sixteen draws. This
is a correctness fix for a real divergence and it is NOT the cause of #194.

Gates, because a lifecycle change reaches every application: darling-testsuite wired 67, ran 67,
passed 66, failed 0, the recorded baseline, and all six roster applications pass the INPUT roster,
which is the criterion a launch sweep cannot establish.
